0,"t":"(2n + 2)(2n + 1) = 2(n + 1)"},{"x":0.74736,"y":0.75742,"w":0.09114,"h":0.02163,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2n + 1 S 1."},{"x":0.36257,"y":0.78907,"w":0.59302,"h":0.03069,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Because the limit is r = 1, we cannot decide from the Ratio Test whether the series converges. When we notice that an+1>an = (2n + 2)>(2n + 1), we conclude that"},{"x":0.39039,"y":0.81001,"w":0.56524,"h":0.05392,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"an+1 is always greater than an because (2n + 2)>(2n + 1) is always greater than 1. Therefore, all terms are greater than or equal to a1 = 2, and the nth term does not approach zero as n S q. The series diverges."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.88275,"w":0.10518,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The Root Test"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.90673,"w":0.58894,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The convergence tests we have so far for Σanwork best when the formula for an is rela- tively simple. However, consider the series with the terms"}]}